Place this as MEMORY.md inside your Claude Code memory directory (the
one your harness auto-loads every session -- check your setup for the
exact path; this is separate from the NOTES.md/ASK.md files in the repo
itself).

MEMORY.md is an index, not the memories themselves -- each line points
to a small file with real content:

- [Title](file.md) -- one-line hook

Keep entries under ~150 characters. When you learn something worth
keeping across sessions (a standing preference, an open project fact, a
pointer to where something lives), write a small file with frontmatter:

---
name: short-kebab-case-slug
description: one-line summary
metadata:
  type: user | feedback | project | reference
---

Content goes here. Link related memories with [[other-slug]].

Four types, roughly: `user` (who's operating this and how they like to
work), `feedback` (corrections and confirmations about your approach),
`project` (ongoing facts/decisions about the work itself), `reference`
(pointers to where up-to-date info lives elsewhere). Don't duplicate
things you can just re-derive by reading the repo (code, git history,
file structure) -- memory is for what isn't recoverable by looking.
